    "s155": "13 · Common shopping questions",
    "s156": "Where can I find less common fruit?",
    "s157": "Start at a produce shop or neighbourhood market; look for frozen pulp for flavours from other regions. Season and stock matter more than shop size. A fruit pictured here may not be available on your visit.",
    "s158": "Is a municipal market the same as a supermarket?",
    "s159": "No. It may bring together specialist stalls, food and different products with a different shopping process. The São Paulo photo illustrates variety; it is not a suggestion to travel there from Rio for groceries.",
    "s160": "What should I buy if I only have a minibar?",
    "s161": "Small portions and ready-to-eat goods. Check space and temperature before buying chilled items; a minibar is not necessarily equivalent to a kitchen fridge and freezer. Frozen pulp may be impractical without a blender.",
    "s162": "What gift would I choose?",
    "s163": "Coffee suited to the recipient and a small selection of Brazilian sweets. I prefer variety and sound packaging over weight. Check travel and destination entry requirements first.",
    "s164": "Should I buy everything on arrival?",
    "s165": "Make a small first shop for breakfast, water and something to try. Once you know your routine and accommodation’s equipment, buy the rest. Avoid duplicate purchases, an overfilled fridge and waste at departure.",
    "s166": "14 · Connect your shop with the rest of Rio",
    "s167": "Recognising these ingredients changes how you read a menu: farofa with meat, guava paste with cheese, passion fruit in dessert or coffee from another region. The supermarket is a starting point; keep discovering Rio in its markets, cafés and restaurants.",
